In lib/File/SOPS.pm around line 2694 (creation_rules_for's path_regex matcher),
the code does:

    my $matched = eval { $subject =~ /$regex/ ? 1 : 0 };   # compiles/matches FIRST
    ...
    my ($construct, $kind) = _re2_path_regex_diagnosis($regex);  # diagnoses AFTER

For a path_regex containing \Q or \E (both dialects accept it, RE2 reads it as
a quoted-literal run, Perl reads it as nothing at all -- see ADR 0048/0051 and
t/22-creation-rules.t "a path_regex both dialects accept but read apart is
refused (k164)"), this order means Perl actually attempts the real match
BEFORE the RE2-divergence scan runs, which triggers:

    Unrecognized escape \Q passed through in regex; marked by <-- HERE ...
    Unrecognized escape \E passed through in regex; marked by <-- HERE ...

on STDERR -- reproducible directly from `prove -lr t/22-creation-rules.t`.

The library still ends up croak-ing with the correct, friendly "read
DIFFERENTLY" message afterward (t/22 passes, full suite is green: 86 files /
1464 tests), so this is NOT a correctness bug. But it's an inconsistency with
the twin check in Metadata.pm's _rule_verdict (used for encrypted_regex /
unencrypted_regex), which runs the RE2-divergence scan (_re2_divergent_construct)
BEFORE ever calling `eval { qr/$pattern/ }` (see Metadata.pm ~line 1640-1666) --
that path never warns, because a divergent pattern returns early and the
compile attempt is skipped entirely.

Fix: in SOPS.pm's creation-rule matcher, call _re2_path_regex_diagnosis($regex)
BEFORE the `eval { $subject =~ /$regex/ }` line, mirroring Metadata.pm's order,
so a divergent/unsupported path_regex never reaches a real Perl regex
compile/match attempt. Keep the existing croak wording and behavior for both
the 'different' and 'unsupported' kinds, and keep the "not a valid Perl regex
at all" croak (currently lines ~2695-2700) for patterns that pass the
whitelist scan but still fail to compile.

Regression test: assert no warnings are emitted (e.g. via warnings captured
with Test::Warnings or local $SIG{__WARN__}) when creation_rules_for is given
a path_regex of \Qa.b\E or a\E, while the existing t/22 assertions on the
croak message keep passing.

Found while investigating a user question about spurious "Unrecognized
escape" warnings in the t/22-creation-rules.t output; full suite run
(prove -lr t/, sops binary present at /home/getty/bin/sops) confirmed
green -- this is cosmetic/order-of-operations, not a test failure.

